1
00:00:01,066 --> 00:00:03,870
♪ ♪

2
00:00:10,643 --> 00:00:13,312
NARRATOR:
Leonardo da Vinci.

3
00:00:13,345 --> 00:00:15,314
Legendary artist.

4
00:00:15,347 --> 00:00:17,250
VINCENT DELIEUVIN:
His genius is universal.

5
00:00:17,283 --> 00:00:18,718
It speaks to everybody.

6
00:00:18,751 --> 00:00:24,190
NARRATOR:
He was also a scientist
and inventor.

7
00:00:24,223 --> 00:00:25,958
KARLY BAST:
There was a reason
for every decision

8
00:00:25,991 --> 00:00:27,427
and every line.

9
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:29,062
NARRATOR: How did he create

10
00:00:29,095 --> 00:00:30,930
the most famous painting
on Earth?

11
00:00:30,963 --> 00:00:34,067
FRANCESCA BORGO:
You can perceive the beating
of the pulse

12
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:35,234
underneath her skin.

13
00:00:35,267 --> 00:00:37,236
♪ ♪

14
00:00:37,269 --> 00:00:42,208
NARRATOR:
The answers have been as elusive
as her smile.

15
00:00:42,241 --> 00:00:44,110
Leonardo embraced mystery.

16
00:00:44,143 --> 00:00:48,147
NARRATOR:
Now researchers are peeling away
the layers.

17
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:51,284
DELIEUVIN:
Thanks to a new kind of
scientific investigation,

18
00:00:51,317 --> 00:00:54,487
we are really able to get
inside the painting.

19
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:58,124
NARRATOR:
Can science unlock her secrets?

20
00:00:58,157 --> 00:00:59,325
MARGARET LIVINGSTONE:
She seems like she's alive,

21
00:00:59,358 --> 00:01:00,359
because she looks different

22
00:01:00,392 --> 00:01:02,228
depending on
where you're looking.

23
00:01:02,261 --> 00:01:07,667
NARRATOR:
And decipher the genius
behind her creator?

24
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:09,735
"Decoding da Vinci,"

25
00:01:09,768 --> 00:01:11,704
right now, on "NOVA."

26
00:01:11,737 --> 00:01:16,242
♪ ♪

27
00:01:22,515 --> 00:01:25,451
♪ ♪

28
00:01:25,484 --> 00:01:29,555
NARRATOR:
In the heart of Paris lies
a former royal castle.

29
00:01:29,588 --> 00:01:35,561
It is now the world's busiest
art museum:

30
00:01:35,594 --> 00:01:38,064
the Louvre.

31
00:01:38,097 --> 00:01:40,867
Filled with glorious galleries

32
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:44,437
of antiquities, mummies,
and Michelangelos.

33
00:01:44,470 --> 00:01:48,041
♪ ♪

34
00:01:51,443 --> 00:01:54,780
But one masterpiece
is the biggest draw of all.

35
00:01:54,813 --> 00:01:57,650
Today's reigning queen--

36
00:01:57,683 --> 00:02:03,256
the "Mona Lisa,"
by Leonardo da Vinci.

37
00:02:03,289 --> 00:02:05,491
WALTER ISAACSON:
The "Mona Lisa" has become

38
00:02:05,524 --> 00:02:10,029
some grand thing
in our imagination.

39
00:02:10,062 --> 00:02:12,198
DELIEUVIN:
Each time you see
the "Mona Lisa,"

40
00:02:12,231 --> 00:02:14,333
you have a different feeling

41
00:02:14,366 --> 00:02:16,503
about what she has in mind.

42
00:02:17,603 --> 00:02:19,272
NARRATOR:
Every year, millions visit her,

43
00:02:19,305 --> 00:02:21,107
drawn by her beauty

44
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:24,877
or, perhaps, her fame.

45
00:02:24,910 --> 00:02:26,779
BORGO:
They've clocked the average
amount the visitors

46
00:02:26,812 --> 00:02:29,182
spend looking
at the "Mona Lisa,"

47
00:02:29,215 --> 00:02:31,451
and that's roughly
about 15 seconds.

48
00:02:33,285 --> 00:02:35,488
You're more focused
on getting a good picture

49
00:02:35,521 --> 00:02:36,489
for your Instagram feed

50
00:02:36,522 --> 00:02:39,325
than actually
looking at the painting.

51
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:43,496
NARRATOR:
So is the "Mona Lisa"
just famous

52
00:02:43,529 --> 00:02:45,631
for being famous?

53
00:02:45,664 --> 00:02:48,734
More icon than art?

54
00:02:48,767 --> 00:02:51,837
Or does she deserve her place
on the Louvre's throne

55
00:02:51,870 --> 00:02:54,440
as the most celebrated painting
in the world?

56
00:02:54,473 --> 00:02:56,642
DELIEUVIN:
It's not just a portrait

57
00:02:56,675 --> 00:02:59,412
of a woman who was living
500 years ago.

58
00:02:59,445 --> 00:03:03,216
It's a demonstration
of how painting could show life.

59
00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:07,853
NARRATOR:
Now researchers are using
new techniques

60
00:03:07,886 --> 00:03:10,556
to investigate the most famous
painting on Earth

61
00:03:10,589 --> 00:03:11,624
like never before.

62
00:03:11,657 --> 00:03:13,993
♪ ♪

63
00:03:14,026 --> 00:03:15,094
MARTIN KEMP:
The smile is crucial.

64
00:03:15,127 --> 00:03:18,564
It's teasing you, saying,

65
00:03:18,597 --> 00:03:20,700
"I know something
you don't know."

66
00:03:20,733 --> 00:03:24,904
NARRATOR:
Could the "Mona Lisa,"
with her enigmatic smile,

67
00:03:24,937 --> 00:03:29,275
be the key to decoding
the man who made her--

68
00:03:29,308 --> 00:03:33,346
Leonardo da Vinci?

69
00:03:33,379 --> 00:03:36,482
No doubt he was
a brilliant artist,

70
00:03:36,515 --> 00:03:40,286
but he was also
a groundbreaking scientist.

71
00:03:40,319 --> 00:03:44,223
He anticipated theories
by both Galileo and Newton

72
00:03:44,256 --> 00:03:46,959
by at least a century.

73
00:03:46,992 --> 00:03:49,262
And dreamed up
remarkable inventions

74
00:03:49,295 --> 00:03:52,898
that seem to predict
our modern age:

75
00:03:52,931 --> 00:03:54,433
armored tanks,

76
00:03:54,466 --> 00:03:56,802
flying machines,

77
00:03:56,835 --> 00:04:01,074
even something
like a self-driving car.

78
00:04:02,708 --> 00:04:05,945
But were
his scientific explorations

79
00:04:05,978 --> 00:04:08,881
a distraction from his painting?

80
00:04:10,449 --> 00:04:15,588
Or was science the secret
to his artistic genius?

81
00:04:15,621 --> 00:04:21,160
Was the "Mona Lisa," in fact,
Leonardo's greatest invention?

82
00:04:21,193 --> 00:04:23,596
ISAACSON:
If you want to understand
Leonardo da Vinci,

83
00:04:23,629 --> 00:04:25,931
you just have to look
at the "Mona Lisa,"

84
00:04:25,964 --> 00:04:27,533
because it's all there.

85
00:04:27,566 --> 00:04:31,570
It's the culmination
of a lifetime spent

86
00:04:31,603 --> 00:04:35,641
loving science and art.

87
00:04:35,674 --> 00:04:42,015
♪ ♪

88
00:04:46,018 --> 00:04:49,755
NARRATOR:
In the back streets
of Florence, Italy,

89
00:04:49,788 --> 00:04:53,893
Valter Conti
and his daughter Elena

90
00:04:53,926 --> 00:04:56,262
prepare for their tribute
to Leonardo

91
00:04:56,295 --> 00:04:57,997
and the "Mona Lisa"...

92
00:04:59,398 --> 00:05:04,437
Human statues
handing out Leonardo quotes.

93
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:08,874
It's their way
of marking the 500th anniversary

94
00:05:08,907 --> 00:05:10,142
of Leonardo's death,

95
00:05:10,175 --> 00:05:13,446
and just one
of many celebrations

96
00:05:13,479 --> 00:05:15,882
that are in the works
around the world.

97
00:05:17,316 --> 00:05:21,087
From a fine art museum
in Beijing

98
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:25,558
to a multimedia extravaganza
in Peru...

99
00:05:26,925 --> 00:05:29,995
And a blockbuster exhibition
at the Louvre.

100
00:05:30,028 --> 00:05:32,531
DELIEUVIN:
For the Louvre museum,

101
00:05:32,564 --> 00:05:34,367
it's a really important moment.

102
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:36,936
Because Louvre
is like Leonardo da Vinci.

103
00:05:36,969 --> 00:05:41,006
We have the third
of all his paintings.

104
00:05:41,039 --> 00:05:45,111
NARRATOR:
Vincent Delieuvin oversees
the five Leonardo masterpieces

105
00:05:45,144 --> 00:05:47,179
that hang at the Louvre.

106
00:05:47,212 --> 00:05:51,250
♪ ♪

107
00:05:51,283 --> 00:05:55,054
That's the largest collection
in the world.

108
00:05:55,087 --> 00:05:56,555
While Leonardo may be

109
00:05:56,588 --> 00:05:59,091
the most famous painter
of all time,

110
00:05:59,124 --> 00:06:03,195
he completed surprisingly few
paintings.

111
00:06:03,228 --> 00:06:05,398
Leonardo was really
someone experimental.

112
00:06:05,431 --> 00:06:06,832
He didn't want to paint a lot.

113
00:06:06,865 --> 00:06:08,801
He wanted to paint
a perfect painting.

114
00:06:08,834 --> 00:06:11,837
♪ ♪

115
00:06:11,870 --> 00:06:13,005
NARRATOR:
So what did it take

116
00:06:13,038 --> 00:06:16,275
for Leonardo
to make a perfect painting?

117
00:06:16,308 --> 00:06:21,514
The answer may be lying
right under Delieuvin's feet.

118
00:06:25,651 --> 00:06:26,719
For the upcoming exhibition,

119
00:06:26,752 --> 00:06:29,555
he is working
with a team of scientists

120
00:06:29,588 --> 00:06:31,857
housed downstairs
from the Louvre,

121
00:06:31,890 --> 00:06:34,293
at the Center for Research
and Restoration

122
00:06:34,326 --> 00:06:37,062
of Museums in France.

123
00:06:37,095 --> 00:06:40,399
DELIEUVIN:
Thanks to a new kind
of scientific investigation,

124
00:06:40,432 --> 00:06:42,968
we are really able to get
inside the painting

125
00:06:43,001 --> 00:06:45,004
and to understand
how Leonardo was working

126
00:06:45,037 --> 00:06:47,106
to perfect the painting

127
00:06:47,139 --> 00:06:51,277
for a long, long time--
during five, ten, 20 years.

128
00:06:51,310 --> 00:06:53,145
It's really something
really specific to Leonardo.

129
00:06:53,178 --> 00:06:57,483
NARRATOR:
They are peering deep inside
Leonardo's masterpieces,

130
00:06:57,516 --> 00:07:01,854
hoping to reveal
the secrets of his technique

131
00:07:01,887 --> 00:07:03,489
that our eyes cannot see.

132
00:07:03,522 --> 00:07:05,691
BRUNO MOTTIN:
The eye sees something

133
00:07:05,724 --> 00:07:08,127
that it believes
to be two-dimensional,

134
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:10,362
but which is really
three-dimensional,

135
00:07:10,395 --> 00:07:12,331
because there is depth
in a painting.

136
00:07:12,364 --> 00:07:14,700
(phone ringing)

137
00:07:14,733 --> 00:07:17,203
NARRATOR:
For art historian Bruno Mottin,

138
00:07:17,236 --> 00:07:18,904
the first step is to understand

139
00:07:18,937 --> 00:07:21,140
the chemistry
of Leonardo's paint,

140
00:07:21,173 --> 00:07:24,410
starting with
the powdered minerals

141
00:07:24,443 --> 00:07:27,313
that were the source
of his colors.

142
00:07:28,413 --> 00:07:30,449
MOTTIN:
You have green, which is made

143
00:07:30,482 --> 00:07:32,418
with the scratching
of copper plates,

144
00:07:32,451 --> 00:07:35,254
you have vermillion,
which is made with mercury,

145
00:07:35,287 --> 00:07:37,957
you have lead white,
which is made with lead.

146
00:07:37,990 --> 00:07:40,726
But these can't be applied
directly on a painting--

147
00:07:40,759 --> 00:07:43,262
it has to be mixed
with something else.

148
00:07:43,295 --> 00:07:44,296
NARRATOR:
These colored pigments

149
00:07:44,329 --> 00:07:47,900
are mixed with a liquid,
like oil.

150
00:07:49,501 --> 00:07:52,972
That gets painted on in layers.

151
00:07:53,005 --> 00:07:57,710
♪ ♪

152
00:07:57,743 --> 00:07:59,845
In a cross-section
of a painting,

153
00:07:59,878 --> 00:08:01,580
you have the base-

154
00:08:01,613 --> 00:08:03,883
which today
is frequently canvas,

155
00:08:03,916 --> 00:08:07,987
but in Leonardo's time,
they used planks of wood.

156
00:08:08,020 --> 00:08:13,292
On that is a coat of white
that can reflect light.

157
00:08:13,325 --> 00:08:16,929
As the artist works,
semi-translucent paint

158
00:08:16,962 --> 00:08:19,899
is built up layer by layer

159
00:08:19,932 --> 00:08:24,336
and then sealed
with a coat of varnish.

160
00:08:24,369 --> 00:08:27,072
In the end,
our eyes see the interplay

161
00:08:27,105 --> 00:08:29,708
between the light reflected off
different pigments

162
00:08:29,741 --> 00:08:32,077
suspended in the layers,

163
00:08:32,110 --> 00:08:36,115
creating depth
and elusive subtleties.

164
00:08:36,148 --> 00:08:37,750
MOTTIN:
Oil is a translucent medium,

165
00:08:37,783 --> 00:08:41,487
which gives to the mixture
a deepness.

166
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:42,955
You can see through
all the layers.

167
00:08:42,988 --> 00:08:45,257
You don't only see
a flat surface.

168
00:08:45,290 --> 00:08:48,127
You have the feeling
of what is beneath the painting.

169
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:53,866
NARRATOR:
Leonardo worked on the
"Mona Lisa" for about 16 years.

170
00:08:53,899 --> 00:08:55,768
Can these
investigative techniques

171
00:08:55,801 --> 00:09:00,673
help reveal, ultimately,
what he was doing all that time?

172
00:09:00,706 --> 00:09:05,077
♪ ♪

173
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:12,651
The "Mona Lisa" began
in Florence, Italy, in 1503

174
00:09:12,684 --> 00:09:15,521
as a commission
from a wealthy cloth merchant

175
00:09:15,554 --> 00:09:18,757
to paint his wife,
Lisa Gherardini.

176
00:09:18,790 --> 00:09:21,527
The word "mona" was
a polite form of address,

177
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:25,965
much like "madam,"
hence "Mona Lisa."

178
00:09:25,998 --> 00:09:29,902
Over time, she became
something much more.

179
00:09:29,935 --> 00:09:34,873
BORGO:
The "Mona Lisa" started off as
a portrait for a merchant's wife

180
00:09:34,906 --> 00:09:38,544
and ended up as a sort of
manifesto, if you want,

181
00:09:38,577 --> 00:09:41,747
of his ability
as a, as a painter,

182
00:09:41,780 --> 00:09:45,784
of his conception
of the world, even.

183
00:09:45,817 --> 00:09:49,555
NARRATOR:
So is this what the real Lisa
looked like?

184
00:09:49,588 --> 00:09:52,024
And how different
does she appear today

185
00:09:52,057 --> 00:09:55,260
from what Leonardo painted
500 years ago?

186
00:09:55,293 --> 00:09:58,731
(device whirring)

187
00:09:58,764 --> 00:10:02,167
To find out, scientists
captured the "Mona Lisa"

188
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:06,005
with an array
of high-tech cameras.

189
00:10:06,038 --> 00:10:09,208
These detect light
in the electromagnetic spectrum

190
00:10:09,241 --> 00:10:13,045
that is not visible to our eyes.

191
00:10:13,078 --> 00:10:16,782
♪ ♪

192
00:10:16,815 --> 00:10:21,020
So just as some cameras
can see wildlife in the dark,

193
00:10:21,053 --> 00:10:22,388
these cameras can help us

194
00:10:22,421 --> 00:10:25,958
see the "Mona Lisa"
in a new light--

195
00:10:25,991 --> 00:10:29,061
literally.

196
00:10:29,094 --> 00:10:32,298
Each image provides clues
about her past.

197
00:10:33,899 --> 00:10:36,235
MOTTIN:
So we have a lot
of different images,

198
00:10:36,268 --> 00:10:38,237
which can tell us

199
00:10:38,270 --> 00:10:39,571
about the structure
of the painting

200
00:10:39,604 --> 00:10:40,973
and the, the way
it has been made.

201
00:10:41,006 --> 00:10:43,876
NARRATOR:
In the ultraviolet image,

202
00:10:43,909 --> 00:10:48,681
blotches of dark blue appear.

203
00:10:48,714 --> 00:10:51,150
These reveal areas of paint

204
00:10:51,183 --> 00:10:54,720
which are not
by Leonardo's hand.

205
00:10:54,753 --> 00:10:58,624
They are modern restorations to
repair damage to the painting,

206
00:10:58,657 --> 00:11:03,562
like this dangerous crack
in the wood base.

207
00:11:03,595 --> 00:11:06,732
And for Mottin,
it reveals even more.

208
00:11:06,765 --> 00:11:09,034
MOTTIN:
It shows us that the painting
is in fact colored

209
00:11:09,067 --> 00:11:11,070
by a greenish and yellowish
varnish

210
00:11:11,103 --> 00:11:13,338
which changes the colors
of the true painting.

211
00:11:13,371 --> 00:11:18,310
NARRATOR:
This thick varnish has
yellowed and darkened over time,

212
00:11:18,343 --> 00:11:24,016
making it difficult
to make out some of the details.

213
00:11:24,049 --> 00:11:26,385
She looks rather like
a plump lady, we should say,

214
00:11:26,418 --> 00:11:28,520
because we do not know
where the arm stops.

215
00:11:28,553 --> 00:11:32,391
NARRATOR:
But this infrared image
clearly shows

216
00:11:32,424 --> 00:11:36,662
this dark area
was once translucent.

217
00:11:36,695 --> 00:11:38,997
Lisa is wearing a veil

218
00:11:39,030 --> 00:11:43,369
that gracefully falls over her
surprisingly slender shoulders.

219
00:11:46,438 --> 00:11:48,507
♪ ♪

220
00:11:48,540 --> 00:11:51,376
To penetrate
the very deepest layers,

221
00:11:51,409 --> 00:11:54,012
the team turn to a tool
more familiar to us

222
00:11:54,045 --> 00:11:56,081
from a doctor's office:

223
00:11:56,114 --> 00:12:00,452
X-rays.

224
00:12:00,485 --> 00:12:03,089
These can reveal
how the painting began.

225
00:12:05,924 --> 00:12:09,027
Most artists at the time
began with a drawing

226
00:12:09,060 --> 00:12:14,366
and then filled it in
with thick paint.

227
00:12:14,399 --> 00:12:17,703
So the X-ray looks like this,

228
00:12:17,736 --> 00:12:20,205
Raphael's "La Belle Jardinière."

229
00:12:20,238 --> 00:12:25,878
The figures started out
clearly defined--

230
00:12:25,911 --> 00:12:27,980
and stayed that way.

231
00:12:28,013 --> 00:12:31,817
♪ ♪

232
00:12:31,850 --> 00:12:35,254
But when Leonardo's paintings
are X-rayed,

233
00:12:35,287 --> 00:12:37,790
the figures often vanish.

234
00:12:37,823 --> 00:12:43,362
♪ ♪

235
00:12:43,395 --> 00:12:46,298
ELISABETH RAVAUD:
Leonardo image are like phantom.

236
00:12:46,331 --> 00:12:49,101
We don't understand
at first sight

237
00:12:49,134 --> 00:12:51,804
what really is on the picture.

238
00:12:54,105 --> 00:12:59,444
NARRATOR:
In the X-ray of the "Mona Lisa,"
there is no clear outline.

239
00:12:59,477 --> 00:13:01,146
Instead, the image evolved

240
00:13:01,179 --> 00:13:04,550
as Leonardo made
continual adjustments.

241
00:13:06,952 --> 00:13:12,157
This also suggests Lisa may not
have looked exactly like this.

242
00:13:12,190 --> 00:13:13,392
ISAACSON:
He keeps it.

243
00:13:13,425 --> 00:13:14,393
He doesn't deliver it

244
00:13:14,426 --> 00:13:15,894
to the merchant
who commissioned it.

245
00:13:15,927 --> 00:13:17,262
Because for him,

246
00:13:17,295 --> 00:13:20,499
it's no longer
a portrait of Mona Lisa.

247
00:13:20,532 --> 00:13:22,267
It is a universal painting.

248
00:13:22,300 --> 00:13:25,170
♪ ♪

249
00:13:25,203 --> 00:13:27,005
NARRATOR:
In the upcoming exhibition,

250
00:13:27,038 --> 00:13:28,340
Delieuvin plans to hang

251
00:13:28,373 --> 00:13:31,310
some of these
scientific masterpieces

252
00:13:31,343 --> 00:13:35,414
along with Leonardo's
original paintings.

253
00:13:35,447 --> 00:13:37,683
The scans prove
an essential point

254
00:13:37,716 --> 00:13:40,185
about Leonardo's artistry--

255
00:13:40,218 --> 00:13:42,855
he painted like no one else.

256
00:13:42,888 --> 00:13:46,425
DELIEUVIN:
Leonardo is one
of the first artists

257
00:13:46,458 --> 00:13:48,026
to be really free.

258
00:13:48,059 --> 00:13:50,529
He felt free to change his mind,

259
00:13:50,562 --> 00:13:53,065
not only during
the drawing preparation,

260
00:13:53,098 --> 00:13:57,803
but also during the painting
of his work.

261
00:13:57,836 --> 00:13:59,705
This is incredibly not common.

262
00:13:59,738 --> 00:14:02,708
He is the only one to give
such liberty, free manner,

263
00:14:02,741 --> 00:14:05,677
in his execution.

264
00:14:05,710 --> 00:14:08,814
♪ ♪

265
00:14:08,847 --> 00:14:11,984
NARRATOR:
Artists then were considered
craftsmen,

266
00:14:12,017 --> 00:14:16,188
and needed to churn out
paintings for patrons.

267
00:14:16,221 --> 00:14:23,762
So how did Leonardo
become such a free spirit?

268
00:14:23,795 --> 00:14:26,899
From the beginning,
Leonardo was an outsider.

269
00:14:26,932 --> 00:14:29,368
♪ ♪

270
00:14:29,401 --> 00:14:33,372
Born in 1452
to an unwed farm girl

271
00:14:33,405 --> 00:14:36,141
in the small Italian town
of Vinci,

272
00:14:36,174 --> 00:14:40,178
he was named
"Leonardo from Vinci."

273
00:14:40,211 --> 00:14:43,081
Therefore,
Leonardo da Vinci.

274
00:14:43,114 --> 00:14:44,850
ISAACSON:
Leonardo had
the great good fortune

275
00:14:44,883 --> 00:14:46,051
to be born out of wedlock.

276
00:14:46,084 --> 00:14:49,588
It meant he didn't
have crammed into him

277
00:14:49,621 --> 00:14:53,592
the sort of old dusty scholastic
wisdom of the Middle Ages.

278
00:14:53,625 --> 00:14:56,161
Instead,
he got to be self-taught.

279
00:14:56,194 --> 00:15:00,165
It also meant he didn't have
to be a notary like his father.

280
00:15:00,198 --> 00:15:01,867
And so he has a fresh life,

281
00:15:01,900 --> 00:15:03,568
where he can be
anything he wants.

282
00:15:03,601 --> 00:15:06,338
♪ ♪

283
00:15:06,371 --> 00:15:08,473
NARRATOR:
At about 14, Leonardo's father

284
00:15:08,506 --> 00:15:11,009
sent him to learn a trade
in Florence,

285
00:15:11,042 --> 00:15:15,314
the epicenter of commerce,
learning, and beauty

286
00:15:15,347 --> 00:15:17,583
at the beginning
of the Renaissance.

287
00:15:20,418 --> 00:15:23,088
PAOLO GALLUZZI:
Florence was one
of the most advanced

288
00:15:23,121 --> 00:15:24,890
social organization
of the planet.

289
00:15:24,923 --> 00:15:27,326
Very wealthy city.

290
00:15:27,359 --> 00:15:30,128
A city that was attracting
the brightest guys around.

291
00:15:30,161 --> 00:15:32,698
(bicycle bell rings)

292
00:15:32,731 --> 00:15:35,167
KEMP:
Florence is just extraordinary
at this time,

293
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:38,570
to produce Donatello, Masaccio,
Brunelleschi,

294
00:15:38,603 --> 00:15:40,672
Michelangelo, Raphael.

295
00:15:40,705 --> 00:15:44,142
♪ ♪

296
00:15:44,175 --> 00:15:45,444
NARRATOR:
With no formal education,

297
00:15:45,477 --> 00:15:47,946
Leonardo started
as an apprentice

298
00:15:47,979 --> 00:15:51,216
in one of the leading
artist's studios of the day:

299
00:15:51,249 --> 00:15:56,021
the workshop
of Andrea del Verrocchio.

300
00:15:56,054 --> 00:15:57,689
Verrocchio
was a sculptor above all,

301
00:15:57,722 --> 00:16:00,492
but also a painter,
an architect.

302
00:16:00,525 --> 00:16:01,660
He could play music.

303
00:16:01,693 --> 00:16:03,762
♪ ♪

304
00:16:03,795 --> 00:16:06,798
So the example of Verrocchio
was very important

305
00:16:06,831 --> 00:16:09,835
to Leonardo.

306
00:16:09,868 --> 00:16:11,336
Leonardo's training
with Verrocchio

307
00:16:11,369 --> 00:16:15,140
is a real model
of how an artist should evolve.

308
00:16:16,441 --> 00:16:18,710
Technique is subordinate
to the act of seeing.

309
00:16:18,743 --> 00:16:19,911
It's about the eye,

310
00:16:19,944 --> 00:16:22,347
and it's Leonardo
who first described that.

311
00:16:22,380 --> 00:16:25,617
NARRATOR:
It is believed
the handsome model

312
00:16:25,650 --> 00:16:28,053
for this Verrocchio statue
of David

313
00:16:28,086 --> 00:16:32,224
is his apprentice,
a young Leonardo.

314
00:16:32,257 --> 00:16:35,660
So, they think that
Leonardo posed for his master.

315
00:16:35,693 --> 00:16:39,731
It's a very elegant boy face

316
00:16:39,764 --> 00:16:41,833
framed
by these beautiful curls.

317
00:16:41,866 --> 00:16:44,803
NARRATOR:
Despite the circumstances
of his illegitimate birth,

318
00:16:44,836 --> 00:16:50,108
the workshop gave Leonardo
a way to get ahead in life.

319
00:16:50,141 --> 00:16:51,777
ISAACSON:
He's a misfit.

320
00:16:51,810 --> 00:16:53,445
He's illegitimate.

321
00:16:53,478 --> 00:16:55,514
He's gay, he's left-handed,

322
00:16:55,547 --> 00:16:57,983
he's a vegetarian,
he's distracted,

323
00:16:58,016 --> 00:17:00,452
and yet he's embraced
by the people of Florence

324
00:17:00,485 --> 00:17:05,424
because it was
a very tolerant city.

325
00:17:05,457 --> 00:17:10,062
NARRATOR:
One of the most challenging
construction projects of the day

326
00:17:10,095 --> 00:17:12,831
was capping the dome of the
breathtaking cathedral

327
00:17:12,864 --> 00:17:15,834
with a golden sphere.

328
00:17:15,867 --> 00:17:19,504
The commission of the crowning
of the cupola

329
00:17:19,537 --> 00:17:21,973
with a gilded sphere

330
00:17:22,006 --> 00:17:23,375
was a very important one.

331
00:17:23,408 --> 00:17:28,380
And that's a commission
that Verrocchio secured.

332
00:17:28,413 --> 00:17:32,417
NARRATOR:
Verrocchio's team, which
included a young Leonardo,

333
00:17:32,450 --> 00:17:35,187
had to figure out
not only the design,

334
00:17:35,220 --> 00:17:38,890
but also how to secure
the one-and-a-half-ton ball

335
00:17:38,923 --> 00:17:43,362
on top of the nearly
370-foot high cathedral.

336
00:17:45,196 --> 00:17:48,233
ISAACSON:
Figuring out how to get the ball
on top of the cathedral

337
00:17:48,266 --> 00:17:50,735
helps Leonardo become
a great engineer.

338
00:17:50,768 --> 00:17:52,270
It helps him become an artist,

339
00:17:52,303 --> 00:17:54,973
because he gets the perspective
of the ball right.

340
00:17:55,006 --> 00:17:59,411
That combination of science,
engineering, and art

341
00:17:59,444 --> 00:18:02,147
becomes part
of who Leonardo da Vinci is.

342
00:18:02,180 --> 00:18:06,852
NARRATOR:
In fact, in sheer quantity,

343
00:18:06,885 --> 00:18:08,453
Leonardo's
scientific investigations

344
00:18:08,486 --> 00:18:10,088
far outweigh
his output of paintings.

345
00:18:10,121 --> 00:18:14,359
The proof of that lies
in a remarkable collection

346
00:18:14,392 --> 00:18:16,528
of replicas of his notebooks.

347
00:18:16,561 --> 00:18:19,531
GALLUZZI:
We have very few records,

348
00:18:19,564 --> 00:18:21,600
in the whole history of science,

349
00:18:21,633 --> 00:18:24,302
similar
to Leonardo's manuscripts.

350
00:18:24,335 --> 00:18:25,937
These are not books.

351
00:18:25,970 --> 00:18:28,173
You cannot compare that
to a work

352
00:18:28,206 --> 00:18:29,774
of, by Galileo or by Newton,

353
00:18:29,807 --> 00:18:32,511
because they have
solved the problems

354
00:18:32,544 --> 00:18:34,379
before writing their books.

355
00:18:34,412 --> 00:18:37,082
NARRATOR:
Paolo Galluzzi is the director

356
00:18:37,115 --> 00:18:39,151
of the Galileo Museum
in Florence.

357
00:18:40,852 --> 00:18:42,087
GALLUZZI:
What we have in Leonardo

358
00:18:42,120 --> 00:18:45,590
is the direct expression
of his internal dialogue.

359
00:18:45,623 --> 00:18:49,995
NARRATOR:
Leonardo's notebooks
span his lifetime.

360
00:18:50,028 --> 00:18:54,266
They even come pocket-sized.

361
00:18:54,299 --> 00:18:57,636
BORGO:
We're sure that
those 6,000 pages that we have

362
00:18:57,669 --> 00:18:59,504
are by him.

363
00:18:59,537 --> 00:19:03,341
And this is just one-fourth
of what he actually penned.

364
00:19:03,374 --> 00:19:06,678
So we're talking about
something he was obsessed by

365
00:19:06,711 --> 00:19:11,049
throughout his life.

366
00:19:11,082 --> 00:19:14,052
NARRATOR:
The brilliance and breadth
of Leonardo's notebooks

367
00:19:14,085 --> 00:19:17,522
is astonishing.

368
00:19:17,555 --> 00:19:21,226
His ideas seem to predict
our modern age,

369
00:19:21,259 --> 00:19:24,029
making Leonardo much more
than a Renaissance scientist

370
00:19:24,062 --> 00:19:25,564
for many.

371
00:19:27,298 --> 00:19:29,968
His dreams of human flight

372
00:19:30,001 --> 00:19:31,870
include a helicopter-like
machine

373
00:19:31,903 --> 00:19:35,040
and a parachute.

374
00:19:35,073 --> 00:19:37,542
A fascination with water

375
00:19:37,575 --> 00:19:40,579
led to ambitious
civil engineering proposals.

376
00:19:40,612 --> 00:19:44,883
♪ ♪

377
00:19:44,916 --> 00:19:48,019
And he conceived an intriguing
self-propelled machine

378
00:19:48,052 --> 00:19:53,659
that seems part-automobile,
part-early robot.

379
00:19:54,993 --> 00:19:58,764
But could any of these inventive
designs have actually worked?

380
00:20:00,865 --> 00:20:02,400
JOHN OCHSENDORF:
Leonardo is, of course,

381
00:20:02,433 --> 00:20:04,536
a great artist,

382
00:20:04,569 --> 00:20:06,471
but he's also a great scientist,

383
00:20:06,504 --> 00:20:08,006
and, I would argue,
a great engineer.

384
00:20:08,039 --> 00:20:10,575
The geometry is stunning...

385
00:20:10,608 --> 00:20:12,911
NARRATOR:
One small sketch
in Leonardo's notebooks

386
00:20:12,944 --> 00:20:16,248
has so intrigued M.I.T. engineer
John Ochsendorf

387
00:20:16,281 --> 00:20:19,651
that he asked
his graduate student Karly Bast

388
00:20:19,684 --> 00:20:23,288
to bring 21st-century
engineering rigor

389
00:20:23,321 --> 00:20:25,924
to see
if Leonardo's 16th-century idea

390
00:20:25,957 --> 00:20:27,959
would in fact stand.

391
00:20:27,992 --> 00:20:30,929
OCHSENDORF:
There is a historical reason
for this,

392
00:20:30,962 --> 00:20:32,130
because you see it
in the drawing, right?

393
00:20:32,163 --> 00:20:33,665
BAST:
Yeah, these two aren't...

394
00:20:33,698 --> 00:20:37,369
NARRATOR:
In 1502, the year before
starting the "Mona Lisa,"

395
00:20:37,402 --> 00:20:39,204
Leonardo proposed

396
00:20:39,237 --> 00:20:41,006
to the sultan
of the Ottoman Empire

397
00:20:41,039 --> 00:20:45,510
a bridge in Constantinople
five times longer

398
00:20:45,543 --> 00:20:48,780
than any other span
at the time.

399
00:20:48,813 --> 00:20:51,149
♪ ♪

400
00:20:51,182 --> 00:20:53,918
His plan
for this ambitious project,

401
00:20:53,951 --> 00:20:56,921
sketched upside-down
in a small notebook,

402
00:20:56,954 --> 00:20:59,224
offers few details--

403
00:20:59,257 --> 00:21:02,894
yet just enough for Bast
to bring it to life.

404
00:21:02,927 --> 00:21:04,963
Leonardo provided
four measurements,

405
00:21:04,996 --> 00:21:07,332
but he provided two sketches.

406
00:21:07,365 --> 00:21:10,368
At first, it seems
like a rough sketch,

407
00:21:10,401 --> 00:21:11,870
but as I dug into it,

408
00:21:11,903 --> 00:21:13,538
I realized
there was a reason

409
00:21:13,571 --> 00:21:16,241
for every decision
and every line.

410
00:21:16,274 --> 00:21:17,976
There was a lot of thought

411
00:21:18,009 --> 00:21:20,845
put into the force distribution,
and it wasn't just aesthetic.

412
00:21:20,878 --> 00:21:23,882
It was engineering.

413
00:21:23,915 --> 00:21:25,750
NARRATOR:
And to test that engineering,

414
00:21:25,783 --> 00:21:27,185
Bast and her colleagues

415
00:21:27,218 --> 00:21:29,554
have built a scale model
of the bridge

416
00:21:29,587 --> 00:21:33,091
with a state-of-the-art
3-D printer.

417
00:21:33,124 --> 00:21:35,460
MOHAMED ISMAIL:
Before 3-D printing came along,

418
00:21:35,493 --> 00:21:36,895
we'd have to try
to build that bridge

419
00:21:36,928 --> 00:21:38,963
as close as we could
to the full scale,

420
00:21:38,996 --> 00:21:41,933
because you couldn't reliably
get the geometry quite right.

421
00:21:41,966 --> 00:21:45,070
NARRATOR:
Leonardo's stone bridge
would have been

422
00:21:45,103 --> 00:21:47,639
500 times
the size of this model,

423
00:21:47,672 --> 00:21:50,709
but the physics
are exactly the same.

424
00:21:50,742 --> 00:21:51,943
BAST:
We have the bridge,

425
00:21:51,976 --> 00:21:54,245
and we have the mold
that's holding it up.

426
00:21:54,278 --> 00:21:56,481
The first piece
should be easy to take off.

427
00:21:56,514 --> 00:22:01,553
NARRATOR:
Piece by piece,
she removes the Styrofoam--

428
00:22:01,586 --> 00:22:04,823
a modern day stand-in
for the wooden scaffolding

429
00:22:04,856 --> 00:22:06,257
used in Leonardo's day.

430
00:22:06,290 --> 00:22:07,859
OCHSENDORF:
This is more like heart surgery

431
00:22:07,892 --> 00:22:09,594
than bridge construction.

432
00:22:09,627 --> 00:22:12,430
NARRATOR:
Finally, the last support
is removed.

433
00:22:12,463 --> 00:22:15,800
♪ ♪

434
00:22:15,833 --> 00:22:20,105
And Leonardo's stone bridge
stands.

435
00:22:21,806 --> 00:22:24,776
OCHSENDORF:
The geometry is
aesthetically beautiful,

436
00:22:24,809 --> 00:22:27,779
and the fact
that this is standing on its own

437
00:22:27,812 --> 00:22:30,181
tells us that it was feasible.

438
00:22:30,214 --> 00:22:31,149
BAST:
Absolutely.

439
00:22:31,182 --> 00:22:32,550
OCHSENDORF:
Wow.

440
00:22:32,583 --> 00:22:35,420
NARRATOR:
Leonardo's bridge even stays up

441
00:22:35,453 --> 00:22:37,355
when they simulate
an earthquake.

442
00:22:37,388 --> 00:22:38,656
Stop.

443
00:22:38,689 --> 00:22:40,125
BAST:
1,500 pounds.

444
00:22:40,158 --> 00:22:41,993
OCHSENDORF:
Oh, my God.

445
00:22:42,026 --> 00:22:45,964
It has moved by 30 feet,
and the arch still stands.

446
00:22:45,997 --> 00:22:50,735
NARRATOR:
The bridge does eventually
collapse,

447
00:22:50,768 --> 00:22:55,507
but only after being moved
the equivalent of 50 feet.

448
00:22:57,241 --> 00:22:59,310
Leonardo has
the artistic ability,

449
00:22:59,343 --> 00:23:01,346
and he also has
that scientific knowledge

450
00:23:01,379 --> 00:23:03,181
and that engineering capability

451
00:23:03,214 --> 00:23:05,316
where he can create things

452
00:23:05,349 --> 00:23:08,019
that are beautiful
and structurally sound.

453
00:23:09,287 --> 00:23:11,923
NARRATOR:
Bast clearly had to fill in
some details

454
00:23:11,956 --> 00:23:14,092
to get from this sketch

455
00:23:14,125 --> 00:23:16,194
to this model.

456
00:23:16,227 --> 00:23:18,630
And we don't know if the bridge
could have been built

457
00:23:18,663 --> 00:23:20,832
with the wooden scaffold
of the day.

458
00:23:20,865 --> 00:23:22,467
And he also shows...

459
00:23:22,500 --> 00:23:23,768
NARRATOR:
But she has shown

460
00:23:23,801 --> 00:23:26,504
Leonardo got the basic physics
right.

461
00:23:26,537 --> 00:23:29,340
OCHSENDORF:
What's extraordinary
is the ideas

462
00:23:29,373 --> 00:23:33,978
that he was coming up with
more than 500 years ago.

463
00:23:34,011 --> 00:23:35,647
Yep.

464
00:23:35,680 --> 00:23:38,950
OCHSENDORF:
We're still trying
to understand them.

465
00:23:38,983 --> 00:23:42,620
♪ ♪

466
00:23:42,653 --> 00:23:45,290
NARRATOR:
As with this ambitious proposal
to the sultan,

467
00:23:45,323 --> 00:23:47,158
Leonardo sought out
wealthy patrons

468
00:23:47,191 --> 00:23:48,726
throughout his life

469
00:23:48,759 --> 00:23:54,299
to support not only his art,
but his scientific explorations.

470
00:23:56,100 --> 00:24:01,473
At 30, he moved to Milan
to work for its duke.

471
00:24:01,506 --> 00:24:04,275
17 years later,
when the duke was deposed,

472
00:24:04,308 --> 00:24:07,679
Leonardo had to move
once again,

473
00:24:07,712 --> 00:24:11,516
back to Florence,
and later, Rome.

474
00:24:11,549 --> 00:24:14,385
His accomplishments
in science and art

475
00:24:14,418 --> 00:24:16,621
meant he had much to offer.

476
00:24:16,654 --> 00:24:18,656
ISAACSON:
Leonardo sells himself
as an engineer,

477
00:24:18,689 --> 00:24:20,391
as well as an artist,

478
00:24:20,424 --> 00:24:22,494
because he knows he can make
a better living.

479
00:24:22,527 --> 00:24:25,930
But also, I think,
Leonardo just loved

480
00:24:25,963 --> 00:24:29,033
the connection
of the arts and sciences,

481
00:24:29,066 --> 00:24:32,237
and he didn't want to have
to be siloed as just a painter.

482
00:24:32,270 --> 00:24:36,674
NARRATOR:
Leonardo's explorations
in science

483
00:24:36,707 --> 00:24:42,113
can also be clearly seen
in the details of his paintings.

484
00:24:42,146 --> 00:24:46,784
He studied everything
from botany

485
00:24:46,817 --> 00:24:50,054
to the physics
of flowing water.

486
00:24:50,087 --> 00:24:54,392
Even the curls
of human hair.

487
00:24:54,425 --> 00:24:55,994
DELIEUVIN:
To paint something
absolutely perfect,

488
00:24:56,027 --> 00:25:01,566
he had to understand
how nature was done.

489
00:25:01,599 --> 00:25:03,935
To paint a mountain,
to paint rocks,

490
00:25:03,968 --> 00:25:07,672
he had to understand
why the rocks were of that kind.

491
00:25:07,705 --> 00:25:09,607
He was obsessed with that.

492
00:25:09,640 --> 00:25:13,111
And that was what he called
the science of painting.

493
00:25:14,779 --> 00:25:19,384
NARRATOR:
The science of painting was
all-encompassing for Leonardo.

494
00:25:19,417 --> 00:25:22,687
But how could he capture
the beauty he observed in nature

495
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:24,856
with paint?

496
00:25:24,889 --> 00:25:29,193
♪ ♪

497
00:25:29,226 --> 00:25:32,797
Back at the Louvre,
art restorers and researchers

498
00:25:32,830 --> 00:25:35,199
are uncovering new clues

499
00:25:35,232 --> 00:25:37,268
in an elegant
intensive care unit

500
00:25:37,301 --> 00:25:40,305
for priceless works of art.

501
00:25:40,338 --> 00:25:44,309
♪ ♪

502
00:25:44,342 --> 00:25:46,544
To prepare
for the upcoming exhibit,

503
00:25:46,577 --> 00:25:47,912
Delieuvin and
the team of scientists

504
00:25:47,945 --> 00:25:51,750
are restoring a painting
long shrouded by controversy.

505
00:25:54,852 --> 00:25:57,455
For centuries,
this portrait of Bacchus

506
00:25:57,488 --> 00:26:01,560
was considered one of the few
paintings by Leonardo's hand.

507
00:26:03,561 --> 00:26:06,264
But now, experts aren't so sure.

508
00:26:06,297 --> 00:26:08,533
(speaking French)

509
00:26:08,566 --> 00:26:10,401
DELIEUVIN:
For centuries, that painting

510
00:26:10,434 --> 00:26:12,870
was attributed to Leonardo.

511
00:26:12,903 --> 00:26:16,207
But during the 20th century,
some historians said,

512
00:26:16,240 --> 00:26:18,376
"Well, it looks like difference

513
00:26:18,409 --> 00:26:19,644
compared
to the other paintings."

514
00:26:19,677 --> 00:26:24,015
NARRATOR:
Cinzia Pasquali has been
brought in

515
00:26:24,048 --> 00:26:26,117
to restore the painting.

516
00:26:26,150 --> 00:26:27,885
Can Leonardo's
unique scientific techniques

517
00:26:27,918 --> 00:26:33,992
provide the clue she needs
to finally solve the mystery:

518
00:26:34,025 --> 00:26:37,562
did Leonardo in fact paint this?

519
00:26:37,595 --> 00:26:40,331
(speaking French):

520
00:26:40,364 --> 00:26:43,868
PASQUALI:

521
00:26:43,901 --> 00:26:46,371
(both speaking French)

522
00:26:47,672 --> 00:26:52,110
NARRATOR:
Restoration work is not
for the faint of heart.

523
00:26:52,143 --> 00:26:54,178
This painting may be
in bad shape,

524
00:26:54,211 --> 00:26:57,515
but Pasquali
is still taking her scalpel

525
00:26:57,548 --> 00:27:02,053
to an irreplaceable masterpiece.

526
00:27:02,086 --> 00:27:05,390
She scrapes away
the yellow darkened varnish,

527
00:27:05,423 --> 00:27:08,993
revealing a vibrant color
beneath.

528
00:27:09,026 --> 00:27:10,995
(speaking quietly)

529
00:27:11,028 --> 00:27:14,065
NARRATOR:
But other parts of the painting
appear more damaged.

530
00:27:14,098 --> 00:27:18,002
So Pasquali must rely
on her training

531
00:27:18,035 --> 00:27:19,671
not only in art history,

532
00:27:19,704 --> 00:27:22,373
but also chemistry.

533
00:27:22,406 --> 00:27:25,309
♪ ♪

534
00:27:25,342 --> 00:27:27,679
The colors of pigment in paint

535
00:27:27,712 --> 00:27:31,616
can change or fade over time.

536
00:27:31,649 --> 00:27:33,951
But the chemistry
of the pigments themselves

537
00:27:33,984 --> 00:27:35,486
can still be detected.

538
00:27:35,519 --> 00:27:41,092
Pasquali orders a special scan
for the element copper,

539
00:27:41,125 --> 00:27:43,228
often used in green paint.

540
00:27:45,096 --> 00:27:48,766
The scan reveals this dark area
of the painting

541
00:27:48,799 --> 00:27:51,269
was once a lush garden,

542
00:27:51,302 --> 00:27:55,106
until the copper in the paint
darkened.

543
00:27:55,139 --> 00:27:56,407
PASQUALI:
So we can see a lot

544
00:27:56,440 --> 00:28:01,479
of vegetable and leaves
and plants and flower,

545
00:28:01,512 --> 00:28:06,217
and when you look on the surface
of the work, they are not.

546
00:28:08,953 --> 00:28:11,889
NARRATOR:
While the beauty of these plants
may suggest

547
00:28:11,922 --> 00:28:14,292
Leonardo's meticulous attention
to detail,

548
00:28:14,325 --> 00:28:16,627
that's not enough to say
for sure

549
00:28:16,660 --> 00:28:18,863
he painted it himself.

550
00:28:20,331 --> 00:28:22,567
So the investigation turns

551
00:28:22,600 --> 00:28:25,303
to his brushwork.

552
00:28:28,339 --> 00:28:31,008
One of the most characteristic
fingerprints of a Leonardo

553
00:28:31,041 --> 00:28:35,046
are remarkably thin layers
of paint,

554
00:28:35,079 --> 00:28:39,050
which can only be seen
with a powerful microscope.

555
00:28:40,618 --> 00:28:48,860
[speaking French]

556
00:28:48,893 --> 00:28:52,430
NARRATOR:
Some Leonardos have been found

557
00:28:52,463 --> 00:28:55,700
to have as many
as 30 layers of paint--

558
00:28:55,733 --> 00:28:58,937
many more than most painters.

559
00:29:01,305 --> 00:29:02,874
As she continues
her restoration,

560
00:29:02,907 --> 00:29:07,078
Pasquali is looking for evidence
of this unusual technique.

561
00:29:08,345 --> 00:29:11,015
But what was Leonardo
trying to achieve

562
00:29:11,048 --> 00:29:12,951
with all those thin layers?

563
00:29:15,252 --> 00:29:17,889
Just ask a living painter.

564
00:29:17,922 --> 00:29:22,193
FLORENT FARGES:
Da Vinci was always looking
for beauty.

565
00:29:22,226 --> 00:29:24,662
He's not just painting
or drawing a tree.

566
00:29:24,695 --> 00:29:29,200
He wants to paint the perfect
expression of what a tree is.

567
00:29:30,734 --> 00:29:34,038
This aspiration
to beauty is, to me,

568
00:29:34,071 --> 00:29:36,574
very inspiring as an artist.

569
00:29:36,607 --> 00:29:38,009
(birds chirping)

570
00:29:38,042 --> 00:29:41,679
NARRATOR:
Florent Farges is a painter
in Dijon, France.

571
00:29:41,712 --> 00:29:45,516
In the tradition
of the Renaissance,

572
00:29:45,549 --> 00:29:47,919
Farges runs
an artist's workshop,

573
00:29:47,952 --> 00:29:49,487
but with a twist.

574
00:29:49,520 --> 00:29:53,024
FARGES:
Hello, everyone,
and welcome to a new video.

575
00:29:53,057 --> 00:29:54,659
NARRATOR:
His is a virtual one.

576
00:29:54,692 --> 00:29:56,794
FARGES:
This one is going to guide you

577
00:29:56,827 --> 00:30:01,966
through the entire process
of classical figure painting.

578
00:30:03,601 --> 00:30:05,837
NARRATOR:
Most Italian painters
at the time

579
00:30:05,870 --> 00:30:08,706
mixed their pigments
into an egg base,

580
00:30:08,739 --> 00:30:13,911
using real egg.

581
00:30:13,944 --> 00:30:15,413
But egg tempera doesn't allow

582
00:30:15,446 --> 00:30:19,217
as much light
to pass through it,

583
00:30:19,250 --> 00:30:23,321
so there is less depth
in the painting.

584
00:30:25,089 --> 00:30:28,459
Instead, Leonardo decided
to use an oil,

585
00:30:28,492 --> 00:30:32,096
like linseed or walnut.

586
00:30:32,129 --> 00:30:34,232
DELIEUVIN:
Leonardo was using oil.

587
00:30:34,265 --> 00:30:38,102
Because oil helps
to reproduce in the best way

588
00:30:38,135 --> 00:30:39,938
the transition
between light and shadow.

589
00:30:42,740 --> 00:30:46,644
NARRATOR:
Gradually, Farges' portrait
comes to life.

590
00:30:46,677 --> 00:30:53,351
♪ ♪

591
00:30:57,187 --> 00:30:58,456
For the finishing touches,

592
00:30:58,489 --> 00:31:00,725
Farges demonstrates
how oil paint

593
00:31:00,758 --> 00:31:02,927
can be applied very thinly

594
00:31:02,960 --> 00:31:04,829
to create
a subtle shading of light

595
00:31:04,862 --> 00:31:07,765
falling across the human body.

596
00:31:07,798 --> 00:31:09,834
FARGES:
If I want, I can come back later

597
00:31:09,867 --> 00:31:11,869
and put another layer
on top of that,

598
00:31:11,902 --> 00:31:14,405
to make this transition
very soft.

599
00:31:17,875 --> 00:31:21,579
NARRATOR:
Leonardo's techniques are still
being taught today

600
00:31:21,612 --> 00:31:24,048
in his hometown of Florence.

601
00:31:25,516 --> 00:31:27,551
To make their paintings
come alive,

602
00:31:27,584 --> 00:31:30,922
artists strive to capture
human flesh

603
00:31:30,955 --> 00:31:34,759
and the way light
reveals its shape.

604
00:31:34,792 --> 00:31:35,960
CECIL:
Leonardo speaks

605
00:31:35,993 --> 00:31:38,196
about the smoky transition
of light to shade.

606
00:31:40,064 --> 00:31:44,035
That's how we perceive
in nature.

607
00:31:44,068 --> 00:31:46,470
The problem often
with photographic images we see

608
00:31:46,503 --> 00:31:48,439
is that there's so much detail,

609
00:31:48,472 --> 00:31:49,641
we don't get the broader effect.

610
00:31:51,342 --> 00:31:56,213
We see life very much
out of focus-- we glance.

611
00:31:56,246 --> 00:31:57,949
NARRATOR:
Other artists of the day,

612
00:31:57,982 --> 00:31:59,617
like Sandro Botticelli,

613
00:31:59,650 --> 00:32:03,087
painted figures
with hard outlines.

614
00:32:05,956 --> 00:32:08,259
But Leonardo used
his fine layers

615
00:32:08,292 --> 00:32:14,231
to create soft transitions,
obscuring the lines.

616
00:32:14,264 --> 00:32:18,769
This is his signature look,
called "sfumato,"

617
00:32:18,802 --> 00:32:22,040
from the Italian "fumo,"
or smoke.

618
00:32:23,974 --> 00:32:26,344
Unquestionably,
the most famous example

619
00:32:26,377 --> 00:32:28,045
of Leonardo's sfumato

620
00:32:28,078 --> 00:32:32,216
is that enigmatic smile
of the "Mona Lisa."

621
00:32:35,085 --> 00:32:38,122
DELIEUVIN:
Just look at the mouth.

622
00:32:41,358 --> 00:32:43,494
Look at the eyes.

623
00:32:45,429 --> 00:32:47,331
You can't see lines.

624
00:32:47,364 --> 00:32:52,503
You just see
the movement of the light.

625
00:32:52,536 --> 00:32:54,772
That's incredible,
and there is no comparison

626
00:32:54,805 --> 00:32:58,909
with other artists
at that time.

627
00:32:58,942 --> 00:33:00,978
KEMP:
There's no edge there at all.

628
00:33:01,011 --> 00:33:03,180
It's all very uncertain.

629
00:33:03,213 --> 00:33:05,483
This plays a psychological role,
of course,

630
00:33:05,516 --> 00:33:08,886
because she is present
but somehow not tangible.

631
00:33:08,919 --> 00:33:12,357
She is idealized.

632
00:33:14,358 --> 00:33:17,962
NARRATOR:
For Leonardo, sfumato captured
in two dimensions

633
00:33:17,995 --> 00:33:22,000
what he observed
in the three-dimensional world.

634
00:33:23,534 --> 00:33:25,536
But in order
to get the skin just right,

635
00:33:25,569 --> 00:33:27,438
he had to go deeper--

636
00:33:27,471 --> 00:33:30,975
to the muscles
and tendons below.

637
00:33:31,008 --> 00:33:33,944
Hold it like that.

638
00:33:33,977 --> 00:33:35,713
NARRATOR:
Trudy Van Houten

639
00:33:35,746 --> 00:33:37,148
has taught anatomy
for 30 years.

640
00:33:37,181 --> 00:33:39,216
Perfect.

641
00:33:39,249 --> 00:33:42,520
VAN HOUTEN:
Leonardo da Vinci constantly
inspires me.

642
00:33:42,553 --> 00:33:46,624
NARRATOR:
She says the science of anatomy
is beautiful.

643
00:33:46,657 --> 00:33:47,625
SYLVAIN:
Would you like me to cut that?

644
00:33:47,658 --> 00:33:48,826
VAN HOUTEN:
Yes, please.

645
00:33:48,859 --> 00:33:50,661
NARRATOR:
Though the process often is not.

646
00:33:50,694 --> 00:33:51,595
VAN HOUTEN:
Nice.

647
00:33:51,628 --> 00:33:53,931
You've beautifully preserved

648
00:33:53,964 --> 00:33:55,333
that tiny little vessel.

649
00:33:56,834 --> 00:34:00,471
NARRATOR:
Leonardo dissected 30 bodies.

650
00:34:00,504 --> 00:34:01,939
And with no refrigeration,

651
00:34:01,972 --> 00:34:05,009
it would have been
especially unpleasant.

652
00:34:05,042 --> 00:34:07,511
The intestines would've been
a particular problem

653
00:34:07,544 --> 00:34:09,413
because they contain
a lot of bacteria.

654
00:34:09,446 --> 00:34:11,582
And even after death,

655
00:34:11,615 --> 00:34:15,119
the intestines become inflated,
and larger and larger.

656
00:34:15,152 --> 00:34:17,421
That's where things
were going to go bad quickest,

657
00:34:17,454 --> 00:34:19,424
and smell the worst.

658
00:34:22,926 --> 00:34:27,065
It's a very messy business.

659
00:34:28,365 --> 00:34:30,101
NARRATOR:
Many of the drawings

660
00:34:30,134 --> 00:34:32,069
from Leonardo's
messy dissections

661
00:34:32,102 --> 00:34:35,272
today live on
in the most refined of places:

662
00:34:35,305 --> 00:34:37,274
Windsor Castle,

663
00:34:37,307 --> 00:34:40,778
just outside of London.

664
00:34:40,811 --> 00:34:43,781
Steps away from where
Prince Harry and Meghan Markle

665
00:34:43,814 --> 00:34:45,783
posed for their wedding photos,

666
00:34:45,816 --> 00:34:52,289
more than 200 Leonardo drawings
are secured in the Print Room.

667
00:34:52,322 --> 00:34:57,161
Martin Clayton is the curator
of those drawings.

668
00:34:58,262 --> 00:34:59,897
CLAYTON:
He couldn't help himself

669
00:34:59,930 --> 00:35:01,632
making a beautiful drawing.

670
00:35:01,665 --> 00:35:04,702
But what most interested
Leonardo was the structure,

671
00:35:04,735 --> 00:35:06,604
the machinery of the body.

672
00:35:06,637 --> 00:35:10,608
And this particular drawing
is one of the finest examples

673
00:35:10,641 --> 00:35:13,344
of Leonardo trying to understand
how the shoulder works

674
00:35:13,377 --> 00:35:15,947
purely in mechanical terms.

675
00:35:18,215 --> 00:35:19,350
There's no mystery of the body.

676
00:35:19,383 --> 00:35:21,418
The body is a machine
that can be looked at

677
00:35:21,451 --> 00:35:24,655
and analyzed
in purely objective terms.

678
00:35:26,690 --> 00:35:31,562
NARRATOR:
The beauty of Leonardo's
drawings is undeniable.

679
00:35:31,595 --> 00:35:33,297
But in light
of all that we've learned

680
00:35:33,330 --> 00:35:36,300
with the help of tools
like the MRI,

681
00:35:36,333 --> 00:35:39,837
did he get it right?

682
00:35:39,870 --> 00:35:43,507
VAN HOUTEN:
If I were asked to grade
his anatomical drawings,

683
00:35:43,540 --> 00:35:45,977
they would go from A to F.

684
00:35:48,178 --> 00:35:49,513
His drawing of the muscles,

685
00:35:49,546 --> 00:35:51,849
of things that he was
directly observing

686
00:35:51,882 --> 00:35:54,018
and giving functional sense to,

687
00:35:54,051 --> 00:35:57,721
those I would give an A-plus.

688
00:35:57,754 --> 00:36:02,860
In terms of some of the organs,
I could not do so.

689
00:36:04,761 --> 00:36:07,798
NARRATOR:
One of Leonardo's most ambitious
anatomical drawings

690
00:36:07,831 --> 00:36:11,135
is called the "Great Lady."

691
00:36:11,168 --> 00:36:13,837
It is considered a masterpiece,

692
00:36:13,870 --> 00:36:17,675
but for Van Houten,
it's a bit of a mess.

693
00:36:17,708 --> 00:36:20,177
VAN HOUTEN:
The feature I went to right away

694
00:36:20,210 --> 00:36:21,779
are strange structures

695
00:36:21,812 --> 00:36:25,983
flying out
from the sides of the uterus.

696
00:36:26,016 --> 00:36:29,520
They reminded me
of carrots with tops.

697
00:36:29,553 --> 00:36:34,291
CLAYTON:
These strange sort of
horn-like structures

698
00:36:34,324 --> 00:36:36,493
are ligaments
observed by Leonardo in a cow.

699
00:36:36,526 --> 00:36:39,230
Leonardo assumed

700
00:36:39,263 --> 00:36:40,865
that all mammals
have the same structures.

701
00:36:42,466 --> 00:36:44,969
He's sort of feeling his way
into a field

702
00:36:45,002 --> 00:36:47,738
that had never
been illustrated before.

703
00:36:47,771 --> 00:36:51,742
NARRATOR:
Leonardo's ground-breaking
dissections

704
00:36:51,775 --> 00:36:54,678
certainly informed his art.

705
00:36:54,711 --> 00:36:56,780
♪ ♪

706
00:36:56,813 --> 00:37:02,219
He returned to this painting,
"Saint Jerome," after 20 years,

707
00:37:02,252 --> 00:37:07,191
revising the neck to accurately
portray the muscle beneath.

708
00:37:12,663 --> 00:37:16,133
He did not revisit
one of his earliest paintings,

709
00:37:16,166 --> 00:37:17,801
"Ginevra de' Benci,"

710
00:37:17,834 --> 00:37:19,036
and it shows

711
00:37:19,069 --> 00:37:24,074
in what has been called
the flatness of her face.

712
00:37:24,107 --> 00:37:26,310
But by the time
of the "Mona Lisa,"

713
00:37:26,343 --> 00:37:31,082
Leonardo's knowledge of anatomy
is beautifully convincing.

714
00:37:33,350 --> 00:37:36,253
One intriguing page
of Leonardo's notebooks

715
00:37:36,286 --> 00:37:39,256
shows several illustrations
of human lips

716
00:37:39,289 --> 00:37:41,158
in exaggerated expressions,

717
00:37:41,191 --> 00:37:45,362
done from dissections.

718
00:37:45,395 --> 00:37:49,900
ISAACSON:
And it shows how each muscle
and each nerve affects the lips,

719
00:37:49,933 --> 00:37:54,204
and at the very top
is this tiny faint sketch,

720
00:37:54,237 --> 00:37:56,373
and you see the first sketch

721
00:37:56,406 --> 00:37:59,410
of what will be the smile
of the "Mona Lisa."

722
00:38:06,249 --> 00:38:08,018
NARRATOR:
Anatomy and his painting
technique

723
00:38:08,051 --> 00:38:11,989
explain in part how Leonardo
infused the "Mona Lisa"

724
00:38:12,022 --> 00:38:15,025
with a lifelike quality.

725
00:38:15,058 --> 00:38:16,560
But to complete the illusion,

726
00:38:16,593 --> 00:38:20,230
he needed to explore
another area of science:

727
00:38:20,263 --> 00:38:24,368
how humans see.

728
00:38:25,836 --> 00:38:30,774
GALLUZZI:
Among the many aspects
in which Leonardo

729
00:38:30,807 --> 00:38:32,209
was using his knowledge
as a scientist

730
00:38:32,242 --> 00:38:34,611
to become a better painter

731
00:38:34,644 --> 00:38:36,413
is optics.

732
00:38:36,446 --> 00:38:39,416
And optics
is called by Leonardo

733
00:38:39,449 --> 00:38:41,118
"perspectiva"-- perspective.

734
00:38:45,088 --> 00:38:46,690
FARGES:
It's basic geometry,

735
00:38:46,723 --> 00:38:49,393
because when you have an object
with parallel lines,

736
00:38:49,426 --> 00:38:53,263
they will seem to vanish
into a point,

737
00:38:53,296 --> 00:38:56,400
which is called
the vanishing point.

738
00:38:56,433 --> 00:38:57,668
NARRATOR:
Leonardo was fascinated

739
00:38:57,701 --> 00:39:01,505
by many aspects
of how we perceive our world.

740
00:39:01,538 --> 00:39:06,009
He even studied
the composition of air

741
00:39:06,042 --> 00:39:07,678
to determine
how atmosphere affects

742
00:39:07,711 --> 00:39:10,948
the appearance of objects
in the distance.

743
00:39:10,981 --> 00:39:13,951
Leonardo is trying to capture
the complexity of the world.

744
00:39:13,984 --> 00:39:19,022
But how can you paint something
that you cannot see,

745
00:39:19,055 --> 00:39:23,827
like the transparency of air?

746
00:39:23,860 --> 00:39:27,231
ISAACSON:
Leonardo not only looked
at mathematical perspective,

747
00:39:27,264 --> 00:39:30,467
but he looked at how colors
change as you get further away,

748
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:32,403
how the sharpness of something
changes

749
00:39:32,436 --> 00:39:34,705
as you get further away from it.

750
00:39:36,907 --> 00:39:40,444
NARRATOR:
And did Leonardo also discover
tricks of perception

751
00:39:40,477 --> 00:39:43,347
to pull off the greatest
illusion of all:

752
00:39:43,380 --> 00:39:44,915
that elusive smile?

753
00:39:49,186 --> 00:39:52,956
She's looking out,
and her smile is a reaction

754
00:39:52,989 --> 00:39:56,093
triggered by the arrival
of somebody.

755
00:39:56,126 --> 00:40:00,664
This is the fiction that
the painting tries to establish.

756
00:40:00,697 --> 00:40:04,468
The great artists know
how to draw you in,

757
00:40:04,501 --> 00:40:06,737
but not to tell you
what to think.

758
00:40:06,770 --> 00:40:07,905
They offer the tease.

759
00:40:09,973 --> 00:40:12,075
NARRATOR:
The ambiguity
of Mona Lisa's smile

760
00:40:12,108 --> 00:40:16,380
is indeed part of her allure.

761
00:40:16,413 --> 00:40:20,184
How did Leonardo
pull that effect off?

762
00:40:22,486 --> 00:40:24,688
That is a question
that intrigued

763
00:40:24,721 --> 00:40:27,858
neurophysiologist
Margaret Livingstone.

764
00:40:27,891 --> 00:40:30,394
She studies
the human visual system:

765
00:40:30,427 --> 00:40:33,464
how our eyes and brain
operate together

766
00:40:33,497 --> 00:40:35,265
to make sense of the world.

767
00:40:35,298 --> 00:40:36,533
LIVINGSTONE:
As a neurophysiologist,

768
00:40:36,566 --> 00:40:39,169
I actually learn a lot
from artists,

769
00:40:39,202 --> 00:40:40,904
because they study how we see.

770
00:40:40,937 --> 00:40:42,072
I study how we see.

771
00:40:43,607 --> 00:40:47,311
A lot of good art
takes advantage

772
00:40:47,344 --> 00:40:50,013
of the computations
your brain makes

773
00:40:50,046 --> 00:40:52,983
by exaggerating things

774
00:40:53,016 --> 00:40:54,518
that your visual system
finds important.

775
00:40:54,551 --> 00:40:59,523
NARRATOR:
Human vision is among the best
in the animal kingdom.

776
00:41:01,658 --> 00:41:03,060
The center of our retina

777
00:41:03,093 --> 00:41:05,662
is packed
with special photoreceptors

778
00:41:05,695 --> 00:41:09,033
that enable us to see details,
or sharpness.

779
00:41:12,202 --> 00:41:15,072
But away from the center--
toward the periphery--

780
00:41:15,105 --> 00:41:17,808
there are fewer of those types
of receptors.

781
00:41:20,277 --> 00:41:24,748
We can see movement,
but not detail.

782
00:41:24,781 --> 00:41:27,084
LIVINGSTONE:
Okay, now I want you
to close your eyes.

783
00:41:27,117 --> 00:41:30,954
I'm going to put up
two version of the "Mona Lisa,"

784
00:41:30,987 --> 00:41:31,855
one accurate and one distorted.

785
00:41:31,888 --> 00:41:33,323
NARRATOR:
To illustrate,

786
00:41:33,356 --> 00:41:35,425
Livingstone enlists
her colleague Peter

787
00:41:35,458 --> 00:41:38,996
to see if he can spot
a fake "Mona Lisa"

788
00:41:39,029 --> 00:41:40,831
using just
his peripheral vision.

789
00:41:40,864 --> 00:41:41,899
Okay, briefly open your eyes

790
00:41:41,932 --> 00:41:43,634
and look at the yellow spot,

791
00:41:43,667 --> 00:41:46,203
then close them right away.

792
00:41:46,236 --> 00:41:50,107
And point to which is
the accurate reproduction.

793
00:41:52,175 --> 00:41:53,677
Open your eyes and see

794
00:41:53,710 --> 00:41:55,880
whether you chose
the correct one.

795
00:42:01,585 --> 00:42:05,756
♪ ♪

796
00:42:10,560 --> 00:42:13,497
♪ ♪

797
00:42:13,530 --> 00:42:15,032
(chuckles)

798
00:42:15,966 --> 00:42:18,435
LIVINGSTONE:
Point at the real version.

799
00:42:21,004 --> 00:42:22,106
(laughs)

800
00:42:23,106 --> 00:42:24,074
Can I keep that?

801
00:42:24,107 --> 00:42:25,209
(laughs)

802
00:42:28,211 --> 00:42:32,215
NARRATOR:
Peter got only one of four
"Mona Lisas" right,

803
00:42:32,248 --> 00:42:36,186
and Livingstone says
that's not unusual.

804
00:42:36,219 --> 00:42:40,190
LIVINGSTONE:
Most people don't know how bad
peripheral vision is,

805
00:42:40,223 --> 00:42:41,992
because as soon
as something happens

806
00:42:42,025 --> 00:42:43,126
in their peripheral vision,

807
00:42:43,159 --> 00:42:44,227
they look at it,

808
00:42:44,260 --> 00:42:45,495
and then they bring
the high-resolution part

809
00:42:45,528 --> 00:42:46,997
of their visual system
onto it.

810
00:42:48,598 --> 00:42:51,501
NARRATOR:
In fact, our eyes are
constantly moving--

811
00:42:51,534 --> 00:42:57,040
three times a second,
filling in the details.

812
00:42:57,073 --> 00:43:00,310
The effect got
Livingstone thinking:

813
00:43:00,343 --> 00:43:01,845
could this explain

814
00:43:01,878 --> 00:43:04,848
why the "Mona Lisa" sometimes
seems to be smiling,

815
00:43:04,881 --> 00:43:06,617
and sometimes not?

816
00:43:08,051 --> 00:43:09,286
Using a photo app,

817
00:43:09,319 --> 00:43:14,224
she blurs the image,
like in our peripheral vision.

818
00:43:14,257 --> 00:43:16,026
LIVINGSTONE:
So I filtered the image

819
00:43:16,059 --> 00:43:17,928
in such a way
that it would look

820
00:43:17,961 --> 00:43:19,363
like what you would see
to your peripheral vision,

821
00:43:19,396 --> 00:43:21,231
knowing what I know
about processing.

822
00:43:21,264 --> 00:43:23,867
NARRATOR:
The result?

823
00:43:23,900 --> 00:43:26,169
LIVINGSTONE:
She's grinning from ear to ear.

824
00:43:26,202 --> 00:43:28,939
NARRATOR:
As you look at the "Mona Lisa,"

825
00:43:28,972 --> 00:43:32,643
your eye moves
around the painting.

826
00:43:34,377 --> 00:43:35,912
When you look away
from her mouth,

827
00:43:35,945 --> 00:43:38,315
it enters
your peripheral vision,

828
00:43:38,348 --> 00:43:43,320
and Mona Lisa
appears to smile.

829
00:43:43,353 --> 00:43:47,691
But look directly at the mouth,
and the smile vanishes.

830
00:43:52,429 --> 00:43:53,797
LIVINGSTONE:
She seems like she's alive,

831
00:43:53,830 --> 00:43:55,499
because she looks different

832
00:43:55,532 --> 00:43:56,934
depending
on where you're looking.

833
00:43:59,969 --> 00:44:01,405
ISAACSON:
Leonardo's paintings come alive

834
00:44:01,438 --> 00:44:03,807
because he understands
human emotion,

835
00:44:03,840 --> 00:44:09,246
and because he has a good feel
for the underlying science.

836
00:44:09,279 --> 00:44:11,214
That combination comes together

837
00:44:11,247 --> 00:44:14,451
year after year as he's doing
the "Mona Lisa"

838
00:44:14,484 --> 00:44:17,587
to make it
such an interactive painting.

839
00:44:17,620 --> 00:44:20,924
(people talking in background)

840
00:44:20,957 --> 00:44:23,093
(speaking French)

841
00:44:23,126 --> 00:44:24,294
NARRATOR:
Back at the Louvre,

842
00:44:24,327 --> 00:44:26,630
Cinzia Pasquali has removed

843
00:44:26,663 --> 00:44:28,432
the old yellowed varnish
from "Bacchus,"

844
00:44:28,465 --> 00:44:34,571
uncovering
the brilliant original colors.

845
00:44:34,604 --> 00:44:37,240
It reveals
an atmospheric perspective

846
00:44:37,273 --> 00:44:40,310
that suggests Leonardo's touch.

847
00:44:40,343 --> 00:44:43,914
DELIEUVIN:
Taking off these layers of
darkened and yellowed varnish,

848
00:44:43,947 --> 00:44:47,250
we were able to rediscover
the original forms,

849
00:44:47,283 --> 00:44:48,952
the quality of the blue.

850
00:44:48,985 --> 00:44:50,120
It's a wonderful blue.

851
00:44:50,153 --> 00:44:53,857
And see how the painter
represent these cities

852
00:44:53,890 --> 00:44:57,160
with that effect of humidity,

853
00:44:57,193 --> 00:45:01,765
of what Leonardo called
the atmospheric perspective.

854
00:45:01,798 --> 00:45:05,068
NARRATOR:
But Pasquali's investigation

855
00:45:05,101 --> 00:45:06,136
has also uncovered details

856
00:45:06,169 --> 00:45:09,005
like the harsh shading
in the face,

857
00:45:09,038 --> 00:45:10,207
that don't show

858
00:45:10,240 --> 00:45:15,378
the characteristic
sfumato fingerprint of Leonardo.

859
00:45:15,411 --> 00:45:16,880
I can't see Leonardo's touch.

860
00:45:16,913 --> 00:45:20,951
This is a little mechanical.

861
00:45:20,984 --> 00:45:25,088
This line for the shadow
is so hard, you know.

862
00:45:25,121 --> 00:45:28,459
Leonardo don't do this.

863
00:45:29,626 --> 00:45:31,394
NARRATOR:
Pasquali and the team
at the Louvre

864
00:45:31,427 --> 00:45:33,997
have solved
a centuries-old mystery.

865
00:45:35,632 --> 00:45:39,703
The "Bacchus" cannot
be attributed to Leonardo.

866
00:45:39,736 --> 00:45:43,707
♪ ♪

867
00:45:43,740 --> 00:45:47,778
But that doesn't mean
he wasn't involved.

868
00:45:47,811 --> 00:45:50,647
Very often, his apprentices are
actually painting the pictures,

869
00:45:50,680 --> 00:45:53,817
so he would conceive
the general design of the work,

870
00:45:53,850 --> 00:45:55,418
and then the manual execution

871
00:45:55,451 --> 00:45:57,988
would be delegated
to members of his workshop.

872
00:45:58,021 --> 00:46:02,659
♪ ♪

873
00:46:02,692 --> 00:46:04,394
NARRATOR:
The restoration has succeeded

874
00:46:04,427 --> 00:46:10,333
in bringing much of the original
beauty back to this painting.

875
00:46:10,366 --> 00:46:13,637
But it also raises
an intriguing question:

876
00:46:13,670 --> 00:46:18,342
should the same thing
be done to the "Mona Lisa"?

877
00:46:19,976 --> 00:46:22,345
DELIEUVIN:
You have to imagine
that under that varnish,

878
00:46:22,378 --> 00:46:25,916
you could see
a wonderful blue sky.

879
00:46:25,949 --> 00:46:29,886
Probably also the face
or the hand are more pink,

880
00:46:29,919 --> 00:46:33,223
like natural skin.

881
00:46:33,256 --> 00:46:37,227
ISAACSON:
If we could just take
that varnish off,

882
00:46:37,260 --> 00:46:40,297
we could see it
the way Leonardo really did it.

883
00:46:40,330 --> 00:46:44,734
But I think French governments
have fallen for less cause

884
00:46:44,767 --> 00:46:45,969
than trying to take the "Mona
Lisa" out of circulation

885
00:46:46,002 --> 00:46:47,304
and clean it.

886
00:46:49,606 --> 00:46:51,875
NARRATOR:
Perhaps there is another way.

887
00:46:51,908 --> 00:46:56,179
Could she be given
a digital makeover?

888
00:46:56,212 --> 00:47:00,917
That is what Pascal Cotte
is trying to do.

889
00:47:00,950 --> 00:47:02,586
He has analyzed
the "Mona Lisa"

890
00:47:02,619 --> 00:47:06,223
with a remarkably powerful
camera and lights,

891
00:47:06,256 --> 00:47:08,625
which he demonstrates
using a replica.

892
00:47:08,658 --> 00:47:11,428
(device whirring)

893
00:47:11,461 --> 00:47:12,762
COTTE:
We make the measurements

894
00:47:12,795 --> 00:47:17,434
in the basement of the Louvre,
inside the laboratory.

895
00:47:17,467 --> 00:47:19,102
It's very emotional,

896
00:47:19,135 --> 00:47:21,471
to have the painting
in your hands

897
00:47:21,504 --> 00:47:22,939
without the frame.

898
00:47:22,972 --> 00:47:24,307
You can look at this painting

899
00:47:24,340 --> 00:47:27,143
under this very intense light,

900
00:47:27,176 --> 00:47:31,681
that reveal everything
that you cannot see usually.

901
00:47:31,714 --> 00:47:36,853
♪ ♪

902
00:47:36,886 --> 00:47:40,123
NARRATOR:
Cotte's extremely detailed scan
of the "Mona Lisa"

903
00:47:40,156 --> 00:47:43,960
and his analysis of the optics
and chemistry of paint

904
00:47:43,993 --> 00:47:48,065
reveal how the colors
may have changed over time.

905
00:47:52,035 --> 00:47:55,538
It's not just the varnish
that yellows and darkens,

906
00:47:55,571 --> 00:48:00,010
but the pigments and oil
in the paint itself.

907
00:48:00,043 --> 00:48:03,947
Cotte's challenge
is to reverse-engineer

908
00:48:03,980 --> 00:48:05,649
the effects of that aging.

909
00:48:05,682 --> 00:48:08,952
KEMP:
This is not just
photoshopping it

910
00:48:08,985 --> 00:48:10,520
and messing around
with the colors,

911
00:48:10,553 --> 00:48:14,591
which you and I could do
and get tolerable results,

912
00:48:14,624 --> 00:48:16,560
but this is based
on pigment analysis.

913
00:48:19,062 --> 00:48:21,965
NARRATOR:
First, Cotte determines how much
the varnish has darkened,

914
00:48:21,998 --> 00:48:27,337
and with his computer,
peels it away.

915
00:48:28,705 --> 00:48:30,073
(whirring)

916
00:48:30,106 --> 00:48:33,810
Next, he identifies
what the color of each pigment

917
00:48:33,843 --> 00:48:36,546
would have looked like
500 years ago,

918
00:48:36,579 --> 00:48:41,685
and recreates them to see
the colors just as Leonardo did.

919
00:48:41,718 --> 00:48:44,754
COTTE:
For example, we know
that Leonardo make the sky

920
00:48:44,787 --> 00:48:47,390
with white lead
and lapis lazuli.

921
00:48:47,423 --> 00:48:50,994
So, we have software

922
00:48:51,027 --> 00:48:53,396
that remove the wrong colors

923
00:48:53,429 --> 00:48:55,499
to obtain the genuine color.

924
00:48:56,733 --> 00:48:59,035
NARRATOR:
Then, pixel by pixel,

925
00:48:59,068 --> 00:49:02,139
Cotte restores those colors.

926
00:49:04,507 --> 00:49:09,779
Suddenly, a greenish sky
becomes brilliantly blue,

927
00:49:09,812 --> 00:49:14,184
and a bit of flush
comes back to Lisa's cheeks.

928
00:49:14,217 --> 00:49:18,355
Finally, as the French say:

929
00:49:18,388 --> 00:49:21,458
voilà.

930
00:49:21,491 --> 00:49:24,928
KEMP:
Suddenly, she doesn't look
like a submarine goddess.

931
00:49:24,961 --> 00:49:26,796
She looks
as if she's in the fresh air,

932
00:49:26,829 --> 00:49:28,365
which is just terrific.

933
00:49:30,733 --> 00:49:34,437
NARRATOR:
Cotte's restoration
has brought Lisa back to life,

934
00:49:34,470 --> 00:49:36,573
at least digitally--

935
00:49:36,606 --> 00:49:41,544
closer to the state
that Leonardo saw her,

936
00:49:41,577 --> 00:49:45,615
allowing us all to see
the painting's legendary beauty

937
00:49:45,648 --> 00:49:49,019
and the science it took:

938
00:49:49,052 --> 00:49:54,090
the geometry
and optics of perspective;

939
00:49:54,123 --> 00:49:57,227
the anatomy behind her face;

940
00:49:57,260 --> 00:50:00,363
and the sfumato soft lines,

941
00:50:00,396 --> 00:50:05,335
capturing the mystery
and movement of life.

942
00:50:05,368 --> 00:50:08,972
But even with
all the modern insights,

943
00:50:09,005 --> 00:50:11,441
the "Mona Lisa" is vastly more

944
00:50:11,474 --> 00:50:16,179
than the sum
of her scientific parts.

945
00:50:16,212 --> 00:50:20,116
Pinning down exactly
why we are so drawn to her

946
00:50:20,149 --> 00:50:25,155
ultimately
remains as elusive as her smile.

947
00:50:29,225 --> 00:50:31,327
NARRATOR:
Three years before his death,

948
00:50:31,360 --> 00:50:33,296
Leonardo was invited to France

949
00:50:33,329 --> 00:50:37,634
to live and work for the king.

950
00:50:37,667 --> 00:50:41,438
He crossed the Alps
by horse or mule,

951
00:50:41,471 --> 00:50:45,975
carrying three paintings.

952
00:50:46,008 --> 00:50:49,379
Those paintings,
including the "Mona Lisa,"

953
00:50:49,412 --> 00:50:51,614
now hang at the Louvre,

954
00:50:51,647 --> 00:50:54,017
where the doors
are about to open

955
00:50:54,050 --> 00:50:58,321
on the 500-year blockbuster
Leonardo exhibition--

956
00:50:58,354 --> 00:51:01,691
a celebration of a genius
who fused together

957
00:51:01,724 --> 00:51:05,628
the worlds of art and science.

958
00:51:05,661 --> 00:51:08,364
DELIEUVIN:
Leonardo is an Italian
Renaissance painter,

959
00:51:08,397 --> 00:51:12,068
but his genius is universal,
and it speaks to everybody.

960
00:51:12,101 --> 00:51:16,206
♪ ♪

961
00:51:16,239 --> 00:51:18,174
BORGO:
Leonardo wanted you to forget

962
00:51:18,207 --> 00:51:21,812
that you're looking
at pigments on a piece of wood.

963
00:51:23,980 --> 00:51:25,348
The idea is

964
00:51:25,381 --> 00:51:30,587
that you're looking at a real
living, breathing being.

965
00:51:30,620 --> 00:51:34,858
♪ ♪

966
00:51:34,891 --> 00:51:37,460
ISAACSON:
The key to Leonardo da Vinci

967
00:51:37,493 --> 00:51:40,230
is that he doesn't make
a distinction

968
00:51:40,263 --> 00:51:42,932
between the beauty of nature
that he studies in his science

969
00:51:42,965 --> 00:51:45,101
and the beauty of his art.

970
00:51:45,134 --> 00:51:51,141
♪ ♪

971
00:51:51,174 --> 00:51:55,545
He could have spent more time
just being a painter.

972
00:51:55,578 --> 00:51:56,980
But had he done that,

973
00:51:57,013 --> 00:51:59,315
he wouldn't have been
Leonardo da Vinci,

974
00:51:59,348 --> 00:52:02,285
and he wouldn't have
painted the "Mona Lisa."

975
00:52:06,856 --> 00:52:09,993
♪ ♪

976
00:52:16,766 --> 00:52:24,307
♪ ♪

977
00:52:28,144 --> 00:52:35,685
♪ ♪

978
00:52:37,320 --> 00:52:44,861
♪ ♪

979
00:52:46,495 --> 00:52:54,037
♪ ♪

980
00:52:59,775 --> 00:53:06,950
♪ ♪

